Reinwald and Leslie M. Shaw and Virginia M.-Y. Lee and M. Korecka and Magdalena Brylska and Yan Wan and J. Trojanowki and S. Neu and T. Foroud and T. Jo and S. Risacher and H. Craft and Liana G. Apostolova and Kelly Nudelman and K. Faber and Zoë Potter and Kaci Lacy and R. Kaddurah-Daouk and Li Shen and D. Soleimani-Meigooni and R. La Joie and K. Chiotis and Maison Abu Raya and Agathe Vrillon and Charles Windon and Julien Lagarde and Zoe Lin and Aidyn Rose Hills and Jason H. Karlawish and Claire M. Erickson and Joshua J Grill and E. Largent and K. Harkins and Zaven Kachaturian and Richard Frank and Peter J. Snyder and N. Buckholtz and John K. Hsiao and Laurie Ryan and S. Molchan and Zaven Khachaturian and M. Carrillo and W. Potter and L. Barnes and M. Bernard and H. González and C. Ho and J. Jackson and Eliezer Masliah and Donna Masterman and L. Ryan and N. Silverberg and Lisa C. Silbert and Jeffrey Kaye and S. Salazar and Aimee L Pierce and Amy Thomas and Tera Clay and Daniel Schwartz and Gillian Devereux and J. Taylor and Jennifer Ryan and Mike Nguyen and Madison DeCapo and Yanan Shang and Lon S. Schneider and Cynthia Munoz and Diana Ferman and Carlota Conant and Katherin Martin and Kristin Oleary and S. Pawluczyk and Elizabeth Trejo and K. Dagerman and L. Teodoro and M. Becerra and Madiha Fairooz and Sonia Garrison and Julia Boudreau and Yair Avila and J. Brewer and Aaron Jacobson and Antonio Gama and Chi Kim and Emily Little and Jennifer Frascino and N. Ferng and Socorro Trujillo and J. Heidebrink and R. Koeppe and Steven MacDonald and D. Malyarenko and J. Ziolkowski and James O’Connor and N. Robert and Suzan Lowe and Virginia Rogers and Barbara Hackenmiller and Brad Boeve and C. Albers and C. Kreuger and David T. Jones and D. Knopman and H. Botha and Jessica Magnuson and J. Graff-Radford and K. Crawley and Michael Schumacher and Sanna McKinzie and Steven M. Smith and Tascha Helland and V. Lowe and V. Ramanan and V. Pavlik and Jacob Faircloth and Jeffrey Bishop and Jessica Nath and M. Chaudhary and M. Kataki and Melissa M. Yu and Nathiel Pacini and Randall Barker and Regan Brooks and Ruchi Aggarwal and L. Honig and Y. Stern and A. Mintz and Jonathan Cordona and Michelle Hernandez and Justin M. Long and Abbey Arnold and Alexandrer Groves and Anna Middleton and Blake Vogler and Cierra McCurry and Connie Mayo and Cyrus Raji and Fatima S. Amtashar and Heather Klemp and Heather Nicole Elmore and J. Ruszkiewicz and Jasmina Kusuran and Jasmine Stewart and Jennifer Horenkamp and Julia L Greeson and Kara Wever and K. Vo and Kelly Larkin and Lesley Rao and Lisa Schoolcraft and Lora Gallagher and Madeline Paczynski and Maureen McMillan and Michael Holt and Nicole Gagliano and Rachel Henson and Renee LaBarge and Robert Swarm and Sarah Munie and Serena Cepeda and S. Winterton and Stephen Hegedus and TaNisha Wilson and T. Harte and Zach Bonacorsi and D. Geldmacher and Amber Watkins and Brandi Barger and Bryan Smelser and Charna Bates and Cynthia Stover and Emily McKinley and Gregory Ikner and Haley Hendrix and H. Cooper and J. Mahaffey and Lindsey Booth Robbins and L. Ashley and Marissa Natelson-Love and Princess Carter and Veronika Solomon and Hillel T. Grossman and Alexandra Groome and Allison Ardolino and Anthony Kaplan and Faye Sheppard and Genesis Burgos-Rivera and Gina Garcia-Camilo and Joanne Lim and J. Neugroschl and Kimberly Jackson and Kirsten Evans and Laili Soleimani and Mary Sano and N. Ghesani and Sarah Binder and Xiomara Mendoza Apuango and Ajay Sood and Amelia Troutman and K. Blanchard and Arlene Richards and Grace Nelson and Kirsten Hendrickson and E. Yurko and Jamie B Plenge and Victoria Rufo and Raj C. Shah and R. Duara and Brendan Lynch and Cesar L. Chirinos and Christine Dittrich and Debbie Campbell and Diego Mejia and Gilberto Perez and Helena Colvee and Joanna Gonzalez and Josalen Gondrez and Joshua Knaack and M. Acevedo and M. Cereijo and M. Greig‐Custo and M. Villar and Morris Wishnia and Sheryl Detling and W. Barker and Marilyn S. Albert and A. Moghekar and Barbara Rodzon and Corey Demsky and G. Pontone and Jim Pekar and L. Farrington and Martin G. Pomper and Nicole Johnson and T. Alo and M. Sadowski and A. Ulysse and A. Masurkar and Brittany Marti and David Mossa and Emilie Geesey and Emily Petrocca and Evan Schulze and Jennifer Wong and J. Boonsiri and S. Kenowsky and Tatianne Martinez and Veronica Briglall and P. Doraiswamy and A. Nwosu and Alisa Adhikari and Cammie Hellegers and J. Petrella and O. James and T. Wong and Thomas Hawk and S. Vaishnavi and Hannah McCoubrey and Ilya M. Nasrallah and Rachel Rovere and Jeffrey Maneval and Elizabeth Robinson and Francisco Rivera and J. Uffelman and Martha Combs and Patricia O’Donnell and Sara Manning and Richard D. King and Alayne Nieto and Amanda Glueck and Anjana Mandal and Audrie Swain and B. Gamble and Beverly Meacham and Denece Forenback and Dorothy Ross and Elizabeth Cheatham and Ellen Hartman and G. Cornell and Jordan P. Harp and Laura Ashe and Laura Goins and Linda Watts and Morgan Yazell and Prabin Mandal and Regan Buckler and Sylvia Vincent and Triana Rudd and Oscar L. Lopez and Ann Arlene Malia and C. Chiado and Cary Zik and Kathleen Savage and Linda Fenice and M. Oakley and Paige C. Tacey and Sarah Berman and Sarah Bowser and Xanthia Saganis and A. Porsteinsson and Abigail Mathewson and Asa Widman and Bridget Holvey and Emily Clark and Esmeralda Morales and I. Young and Kevin Hopkins and K. Martin and N. Kowalski and Rebecca Hunt and Roberta Calzavara and Russell Kurvach and Stephen D'Ambrosio and G. Thai and B. Vides and Brigit Lieb and Catherine McAdams-Ortiz and Cyndy Toso and Ivan Mares and Kathryn Moorlach and Luter Liu and Maria Corona and Mary Nguyen and Melanie B Tallakson and Michelle McDonnell and Milagros Rangel and Neetha Basheer and Patricia Place and Romina Romero and S. Tam and T. Nguyen and Abey Thomas and Alexander Frolov and Alka Khera and Amy Browning and B. Kelley and C. Dawson and D. Mathews and Elaine Most and E. Phillips and Lynn G. Nguyen and Maribel Nunez and Matalin Miller and Matthew R. Jones and Natalie Martinez and Rebecca Logan and R. McColl and Sari Pham and Tiffani Fox and Tracey Moore and A. Levey and Abby Brown and Andrea Kippels and Ashton Ellison and C. Lyons and Chadwick M. Hales and Cindy Parry and Courtney Williams and Elizabeth McCorkle and Guy Harris and Heather Rose and Inara Jooma and Jahmila Al‐Amin and J. Lah and James Webster and Jessica Swiniarski and Latasha Chapman and Laura Donnelly and Lauren Mariotti and Mary C. Locke and Phyllis Vaughn and Rachael Penn and S. Carpentier and Samira Yeboah and Sarah Basadre and Sarah Malakauskas and Stefka Lyron and Tara Villinger and Terra Burney and Jeffrey M. Burns and Ala Abusalim and Alexandra Dahlgren and Alexandria Montero},
  year = {2026},
  abstract = {Cortical thinning and atrophy are hallmarks of brain aging that have been characterized using magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). Brain aging involves many neuroanatomic features whose effects on brain structure remain unexplored. To address this challenge, we trained interpretable deep neural networks (DNNs) to estimate brain age (BA) from T1-weighted (T1w) MRI. By identifying MRI features unapparent to humans, DNNs can find aging-related structural alterations above and beyond cortical thickness and atrophy. Using a novel approach to DNN interpretability, we mapped brain aging progression in 25,539 cognitively normal UK Biobank adults aged 45-83 years. Cortical aging is found to involve anatomic features becoming prominent during the 50s within frontolateral, mesolimbic, cuneal, and occipitotemporal regions. From these foci, aging-related features propagate to adjacent areas at rates peaking in the 60s. Cortical thinning and atrophy do not trend closely with neurodegeneration, but important DNN-identifiable anatomic features have spatiotemporal dynamics that match those of amyloid or tau. Our results challenge the assumption that MRI cannot map anatomic features trending with neurodegeneration. Interpretable DNNs can empower MRI to quantify anatomic aging as a process of spatial feature expansion from focal regions into nearby structures in the sequence of neurodegenerative pathology. Traditional morphometrics explain only ∼1% of variance in DNN-identifiable features, which clarifies why the former are insensitive to anatomic changes involving neurodegeneration. Our results conceptualize brain aging in the context of spatial and temporal parallels between anatomic senescence and neuropathology. These findings may help to map cognitively normal adults' neurodegenerative anatomy even without PET measurements.},
  doi = {10.1007/s11357-026-02112-2},
  pmid = {41984127},
  month = {04},
  journal = {GeroScience},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
  pdf = {https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11357-026-02112-2.pdf},
}

@article{lyu2026medial,
  title = {Medial temporal lobe Tau-Neurodegeneration mismatch from structural imaging and plasma biomarkers},
  author = {X. Lyu and N. S. Mundada and C. A. Brown and N. Sadeghpour and E. McGrew and Long Xie and Yue Li and A. Wuestefeld and M. Duong and P. Cook and J. Gee and I. Nasrallah and Alice S Chen-Plotkin and Leslie M. Shaw and Dawn Mechanic-Hamilton and L. Wisse and Paul A. Yushkevich and Sandhitsu R Das and David A. Wolk},
  year = {2026},
  abstract = {While tau pathology is closely associated with neurodegeneration in Alzheimer’s disease (AD), our prior work using multi-modality imaging revealed that mismatch between tau (T) and neurodegeneration (N) may reflect contributions from non-AD processes. The medial temporal lobe (MTL), an early site of AD pathology, is also a common target of co-pathologies such as limbic-predominant age-related TDP-43 encephalopathy neuropathologic change (LATE-NC), often following an anterior–posterior atrophy gradient. Given the susceptibility of MTL to co-pathologies, here we explored T-N mismatch specifically within MTL using plasma ptau217 and MTL morphometry for identifying vulnerabilities and resilience in cognitively impaired or unimpaired AD patients. We parcellated the MTL into 100 spatially contiguous segments and calculated their T-N mismatch using plasma ptau217 as a measure for T and thickness as a marker of N. Based on these mismatch profiles, we clustered 447 amyloid-positive individuals from ADNI cohort into data-driven T-N phenotypes. We characterized the T-N phenotypes by examining their cross-sectional and longitudinal atrophy both within the MTL and across the whole brain, as well as cognitive trajectories. This framework was replicated in an independent cohort and finally translated to a real-world clinical sample of 50 patients undergoing anti-amyloid therapy. Clustering identified three T-N phenotypes with different MTL T-N mismatch profiles, atrophy patterns, and cognitive outcomes, despite comparable AD severity. The “canonical” group, characterized by low T-N residuals (N ~ T), showed AD-like neurodegeneration patterns. The “vulnerable” group, characterized by disproportionately greater neurodegeneration than tau (N > T), showed atrophy primarily in the anterior MTL that extended into temporal-limbic regions, both in cross-sectional and longitudinal analyses. This group also exhibited neurodegeneration that preceded estimated tau onset and experienced faster cognitive decline across multiple domains, aligning with the typical characteristics of mixed LATE-NC with AD. In contrast, the “resilient” group (N < T) showed minimal atrophy and preserved cognitive function. These phenotypes were reproducible in an independent research cohort. Importantly, in a feasibility study applying the model developed from ADNI to a clinical cohort of patients receiving lecanemab, we identified vulnerable individuals with LATE-like atrophy patterns. This highlights its potential utility for identifying individuals with co-pathology in clinical settings. Our findings demonstrate that T-N mismatch within MTL using MRI and plasma biomarkers can reveal AD groups with varying vulnerability/resilience, with the vulnerable group displaying structural and cognitive outcomes suggestive of LATE-NC. This approach offers a cost-effective strategy for clinical trial stratification and precision medicine for AD therapeutics.},
  doi = {10.1093/brain/awag105},
  pmid = {42046390},
  month = {04},
  journal = {Brain : a journal of neurology},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
  pdf = {https://doi.org/10.1093/brain/awag105},
}

@article{beltranurbano2026ichor,
  title = {ICHOR: A Robust Representation Learning Approach for ASL CBF Maps with Self-Supervised Masked Autoencoders},
  author = {Xavier Beltran-Urbano and Yiran Li and Xinglin Zeng and Katie Jobson and Manuel Taso and Christopher A. Brown and D. Wolk and Corey T. McMillan and Ilya M. Nashrallah and Paul Yushkevich and Ze Wang and John A. Detre and Sudipto Dolui},
  year = {2026},
  abstract = {Arterial spin labeling (ASL) perfusion MRI allows direct quantification of regional cerebral blood flow (CBF) without exogenous contrast, enabling noninvasive measurements that can be repeated without constraints imposed by contrast injection. ASL is increasingly acquired in research studies and clinical MRI protocols. Building on successes in structural imaging, recent efforts have implemented deep learning based methods to improve image quality, enable automated quality control, and derive robust quantitative and predictive biomarkers with ASL derived CBF. However, progress has been limited by variable image quality, substantial inter-site, vendor and protocol differences, and limited availability of labeled datasets needed to train models that generalize across cohorts. To address these challenges, we introduce ICHOR, a self supervised pre-training approach for ASL CBF maps that learns transferable representations using 3D masked autoencoders. ICHOR is pretrained via masked image modeling using a Vision Transformer backbone and can be used as a general-purpose encoder for downstream ASL tasks. For pre-training, we curated one of the largest ASL datasets to date, comprising 11,405 ASL CBF scans from 14 studies spanning multiple sites and acquisition protocols. We evaluated the pre-trained ICHOR encoder on three downstream diagnostic classification tasks and one ASL CBF map quality prediction regression task. Across all evaluations, ICHOR outperformed existing neuroimaging self-supervised pre-training methods adapted to ASL. Pre-trained weights and code will be made publicly available.},
  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2603.05247},
  arxiv = {2603.05247},
  month = {03},
  journal = {ArXiv},
  volume = {abs/2603.05247},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
}

