oo and E. Teng and N. Graff-Radford and F. Parfitt and K. Poki-Walker and M. Farlow and A. Hake and Brandy R. Matthews and J. Brosch and S. Herring and C. V. van Dyck and A. Mecca and S. Good and M. Macavoy and Richard E. Carson and P. Varma and H. Chertkow and S. Vaitekunis and C. Hosein and Sandra Black and B. Stefanovic and C. Heyn and G. Hsiung and Ellen J. Kim and B. Mudge and Vesna Sossi and H. Feldman and M. Assaly and E. Finger and S. Pasternak and I. Rachinsky and Andrew Kertesz and D. Drost and J. Rogers and I. Grant and B. Muse and E. Rogalski and Jordan Robson and M. Mesulam and D. Kerwin and Chuang‐Kuo Wu and N. Johnson and K. Lipowski and Sandra Weintraub and B. Bonakdarpour and N. Pomara and R. Hernando and A. Sarrael and H. Rosen and Bruce L. Miller and David Perry and R. Turner and K. Johnson and B. Reynolds and Kelly McCann and J. Poe and R. Sperling and K. Johnson and G. Marshall and C. Belden and A. Atri and B. Spann and K. Clark and E. Zamrini and M. Sabbagh and R. Killiany and Robert A. Stern and J. Mez and N. Kowall and A. Budson and T. Obisesan and Oyonumo E. Ntekim and S. Wolday and J. Khan and E. Nwulia and Sheeba Nadarajah and Alan J. Lerner and P. Ogrocki and Curtis Tatsuoka and P. Fatica and Evan F. Fletcher and Pauline Maillard and J. Olichney and C. Decarli and O. Carmichael and V. Bates and H. Capote and M. Rainka and M. Borrie and T. Lee and R. Bartha and Sterling C. Johnson and Sanjay Asthana and C. Carlsson and A. Perrin and A. Burke and D. Scharre and M. Kataki and R. Tarawneh and B. Kelley and D. Hart and E. Zimmerman and D. Celmins and Delwyn D. Miller and L. Ponto and K. Smith and H. Koleva and H. Shim and K. W. Nam and Susan K. Schultz and J. Williamson and S. Craft and J. Cleveland and Mia Yang and K. Sink and Brian R. Ott and J. Drake and G. Tremont and Lori A. Daiello and J. Drake and M. Sabbagh and Aaron Ritter and C. Bernick and D. Munic and A. Mintz and Abigail O’Connelll and Jacobo Mintzer and A. Wiliams and J. Masdeu and Jiong Shi and Angelica Garcia and M. Sabbagh and Paul Newhouse and S. Potkin and S. Salloway and P. Malloy and S. Correia and S. Kittur and Godfrey D. Pearlson and K. Blank and K. Anderson and L. Flashman and M. Seltzer and M. Hynes and R. Santulli and N. Relkin and Gloria C. Chiang and Michael P. Lin and L. Ravdin and Athena Lee and Ron Petersen and Thomas C. Neylan and J. Grafman and T. Montine and Sarah Danowski and Catherine Nguyen-Barrera and Shannon Finley and Danielle J Harvey and Michael Donohue and M. Bernstein and Normal L. Foster and T. Foroud and S. Potkin and Li Shen and K. Faber and Sungeun Kim and K. Nho and K. Wilmes and H. Vanderswag and A. Fleisher and Ajay Sood and K. Blanchard and Debra A. Fleischman and Maria T. Greig and B. Goldstein and K. Martin and G. Thai and Aimee L Pierce and Christopher Reist and B. Yáñez and E. Sosa and M. Witbracht and C. Sadowsky and Walter Martínez and Teresa Villena and H. Rosen and E. Peskind and E. Petrie and Gail Li and J. Yesavage and Joy Taylor and S. Chao and J. Coleman and Jessica D. White and B. Lane and A. Rosen and Jared S. Tinklenberg and G. Jimenez-Maggiora and Erin Drake and Michael Donohue and Craig Nelson and David Bickford and Meryl A. Butters and M. Zmuda and Bret J. Borowski and J. Gunter and M. Senjem and K. Kantarci and C. Ward and Denise A. Reyes and K. Faber and Kelly Nudelman and Yiu Ho Au and Kelly Scherer and Daniel Catalinotto and Samuel Stark and Elise Ong and Dariella Fernandez},
  year = {2025},
  abstract = {Progranulin (PGRN) and soluble triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells-2 (sTREM2) are emerging biomarkers of Alzheimer’s disease (AD). This study explores the roles of their interplay in modulating amyloid pathology. We analyzed data from 905 participants (mean age = 62.0) in the CABLE cohort and 973 participants (mean age = 73.1) in the ADNI, classified using the A/T/N biomarker framework. One-way ANOVA was used to assess whether cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) PGRN and sTREM2 differed across biomarker profiles and clinical stages. Multiple linear regression models and linear mixed-effects models were used to test the relationships among PGRN, sTREM2, and CSF Aβ1–42 levels. Mediation analysis was used to explore the reciprocal relationships between sTREM2 and PGRN in influencing amyloid pathology. CSF proteomic and bioinformatic analyses were finally used to investigate the underlying biological mechanisms. In both cohorts, PGRN and sTREM2 were higher in individuals within the TN+ profile irrespective of the A status, and followed similar trajectory across different clinical and biomarker stage. CSF PGRN was associated with higher sTREM2 across AD continuum and non-AD pathophysiology. Bidirectional mediation was observed between PGRN (14.6% in CABLE, 15.6% in ADNI) and sTREM2 (29.7% in CABLE, 33.5% in ADNI) in modulating Aβ pathology (p < 0.0001). Proteomic analysis identified 1539 CSF proteins (Bonferroni-corrected p < 7.13 × 10−6) simultaneously associated with PGRN, sTREM2, and Aβ1–42. These proteins are mainly enriched in immune processes and neural plasticity. These findings suggest that the interplay between lysosome function and microglia-related neuroinflammation plays key roles in amyloid metabolism.},
  doi = {10.1038/s41398-025-03457-6},
  pmid = {40628712},
  month = {07},
  journal = {Translational Psychiatry},
  volume = {15},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
  note = {Cited by 5},
  pdf = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-025-03457-6.pdf},
}

