nd L. Hergesheimer and Stephanie Smith and O. Adegoke and P. Mahboubi and Shelley Moore and J. Pizzola and E. Shaffer and B. Sloan and Danielle J Harvey and A. Forghanian-Arani and Bret J. Borowski and C. Ward and C. Schwarz and David Jones and J. Gunter and K. Kantarci and M. Senjem and P. Vemuri and Robert I. Reid and Nick C. Fox and Ian Malone and Paul Thompson and S. Thomopoulos and T. Nir and N. Jahanshad and C. Decarli and A. Knaack and E. Fletcher and D. Tosun-Turgut and Stephanie Rossi Chen and M. Choe and K. Crawford and Paul Yushkevich and Sandhitsu R. Das and R. Koeppe and E. Reiman and Kewei Chen and C. Mathis and Susan M. Landau and N. Cairns and E. Householder and E. Franklin and H. Bernhardt and L. taylor-Reinwald and M. Korecka and M. Figurski and S. Neu and L. Apostolova and Li Shen and T. Foroud and K. Nudelman and K. Faber and K. Wilmes and L. Thal and Lisa C Silbert and B. Lind and R. Crissey and Jeffrey A. Kaye and R. Carter and S. Dolen and Joseph Quinn and Lon S. Schneider and S. Pawluczyk and M. Becerra and L. Teodoro and K. Dagerman and B. Spann and H. Vanderswag and J. Ziolkowski and J. Heidebrink and L. Zbizek-Nulph and J. Lord and S. Mason and C. Albers and D. Knopman and K. Johnson and J. Villanueva-Meyer and V. Pavlik and N. Pacini and Ashley Lamb and J. S. Kass and Rachelle S. Doody and V. Shibley and M. Chowdhury and S. Rountree and M. Dang and Y. Stern and L. Honig and A. Mintz and B. Ances and D. Winkfield and M. Carroll and G. Stobbs-Cucchi and A. Oliver and M. Creech and M. Mintun and S. Schneider and D. Geldmacher and M. Love and R. Griffith and D. Clark and J. Brockington and D. Marson and H. Grossman and Martin A. Goldstein and J. Greenberg and Effie M. Mitsis and R. Shah and Melissa Lamar and P. Samuels and R. Duara and M. Greig‐Custo and R. Rodriguez and Marilyn S. Albert and C. Onyike and L. Farrington and S. Rudow and R. Brichko and S. Kielb and Amanda G. Smith and B. A. Raj and K. Fargher and M. Sadowski and Thomas Wisniewski and Melanie Shulman and A. Faustin and J. Rao and K. M. Castro and A. Ulysse and Shannon Chen and M. O. Sheikh and J. Singleton-Garvin and J. Petrella and O. James and T. Wong and Salvador Borges-Neto and Jason H. Karlawish and D. Wolk and S. Vaishnavi and Christopher M. Clark and S. Arnold and Charles D. Smith and G. Jicha and F. Raslau and Oscar L. Lopez and M. Oakley and D. Simpson and A. Porsteinsson and K. Martin and N. Kowalski and M. Keltz and B. Goldstein and K. Makino and M. Ismail and C. Brand and G. Thai and A. Pierce and B. Yáñez and E. Sosa and M. Witbracht and Brendan Kelley and Trung Nguyen and K. Womack and D. Mathews and M. Quiceno and A. Levey and J. Lah and I. Hajjar and J. Cellar and Jeffrey M. Burns and R. Swerdlow and William M. Brooks and D. Silverman and S. Kremen and L. Apostolova and K. Tingus and P. Lu and G. Bartzokis and E. Woo and E. Teng and N. Graff-Radford and F. Parfitt and K. Poki-Walker and M. Farlow and A. Hake and B. Matthews and J. Brosch and S. Herring and Christopher H. van and A. Mecca and S. Good and M. Macavoy and Richard E. Carson and P. Varma and H. Chertkow and S. Vaitekunis and C. Hosein and Sandra E. Black and B. Stefanovic and Chris Heyn and Ging‐Yuek Robin Hsiung and Ellen J. Kim and B. Mudge and Vesna Sossi and H. Feldman and M. Assaly and E. Finger and S. Pasternak and I. Rachinsky and Andrew Kertesz and D. Drost and J. Rogers and I. Grant and B. Muse and E. Rogalski and Jordan Robson and M. Mesulam and D. Kerwin and Chuang‐Kuo Wu and N. Johnson and K. Lipowski and Sandra Weintraub and B. Bonakdarpour and N. Pomara and R. Hernando and A. Sarrael and H. Rosen and Bruce L. Miller and David Perry and Raymond Scott Turner and K. Johnson and B. Reynolds and Kelly McCann and J. Poe and R. Sperling and K. Johnson and G. Marshall and J. Yesavage and Joy Taylor and S. Chao and J. Coleman and Jessica D. White and B. Lane and A. Rosen and Jared S. Tinklenberg and C. Belden and A. Atri and K. Clark and E. Zamrini and Marwan Sabbagh and R. Killiany and R. Stern and J. Mez and N. Kowall and A. Budson and T. Obisesan and Oyonumo E. Ntekim and S. Wolday and J. Khan and E. Nwulia and Sheeba Nadarajah and Alan J. Lerner and P. Ogrocki and C. Tatsuoka and P. Fatica and P. Maillard and J. Olichney and O. Carmichael and V. Bates and H. Capote and M. Rainka and M. Borrie and T. Lee and Dr Rob Bartha and S. Johnson and Sanjay Asthana and C. Carlsson and A. Perrin and A. Burke and D. Scharre and M. Kataki and R. Tarawneh and D. Hart and E. Zimmerman and D. Celmins and Delwyn D. Miller and Laura L. BolesPonto and K. Smith and H. Koleva and H. Shim and K. W. Nam and Susan K. Schultz and J. Williamson and S. Craft and J. Cleveland and Mia Yang and K. Sink and B. Ott and J. Drake and G. Tremont and L. Daiello and J. Drake and Aaron Ritter and C. Bernick and D. Munic and Abigail O’Connelll and Jacobo Mintzer and A. Wiliams and J. Masdeu and Jiong Shi and Angelica Garcia and P. Newhouse and S. Potkin and S. Salloway and P. Malloy and S. Correia and S. Kittur and Godfrey D. Pearlson and K. Blank and K. Anderson and L. Flashman and M. Seltzer and M. Hynes and R. Santulli and N. Relkin and Gloria C. Chiang and Athena Lee and Michael P. Lin and L. Ravdin and Ron Petersen and Thomas C. Neylan and J. Grafman and Sarah Danowski and Catherine Nguyen-Barrera and J. Hayes and Shannon Finley and M. Bernstein and M. Senjem and Normal L. Foster and Sungeun Kim and Ajay Sood and K. Blanchard and Debra A. Fleischman and K. Arfanakis and D. Varon and Maria T. Greig and J. Petrella and B. Goldstein and K. Martin and Christopher Reist and C. Sadowsky and Walter Martínez and Teresa Villena and H. Rosen and G. Marshall and E. Peskind and E. Petrie and Gail Li and Scott Mackin and G. Jimenez-Maggiora and Erin Drake and Michael Donohue and Craig Nelson and David Bickford and Meryl A. Butters and M. Zmuda and Denise A. Reyes and K. Faber and K. Nudelman and Yiu Ho Au and Kelly Scherer and Daniel Catalinotto and Samuel Stark and Elise Ong and Dariella Fernandez},
  year = {2023},
  abstract = {Summary Background Deep learning has shown potential in various scientific domains but faces challenges when applied to complex, high-dimensional multi-omics data. Alzheimer's Disease (AD) is a neurodegenerative disorder that lacks targeted therapeutic options. This study introduces the Circular-Sliding Window Association Test (c-SWAT) to improve the classification accuracy in predicting AD using serum-based metabolomics data, specifically lipidomics. Methods The c-SWAT methodology builds upon the existing Sliding Window Association Test (SWAT) and utilizes a three-step approach: feature correlation analysis, feature selection, and classification. Data from 997 participants from the Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI) served as the basis for model training and validation. Feature correlations were analyzed using Weighted Gene Co-expression Network Analysis (WGCNA), and Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) were employed for feature selection. Random Forest was used for the final classification. Findings The application of c-SWAT resulted in a classification accuracy of up to 80.8% and an AUC of 0.808 for distinguishing AD from cognitively normal older adults. This marks a 9.4% improvement in accuracy and a 0.169 increase in AUC compared to methods without c-SWAT. These results were statistically significant, with a p-value of 1.04 × 10ˆ-4. The approach also identified key lipids associated with AD, such as Cer(d16:1/22:0) and PI(37:6). Interpretation Our results indicate that c-SWAT is effective in improving classification accuracy and in identifying potential lipid biomarkers for AD. These identified lipids offer new avenues for understanding AD and warrant further investigation. Funding The specific funding of this article is provided in the acknowledgements section.},
  doi = {10.1016/j.ebiom.2023.104820},
  pmid = {37806288},
  month = {10},
  journal = {eBioMedicine},
  volume = {97},
  altmetric = {true},
