u and G. Bartzokis and E. Woo and E. Teng and N. Graff-Radford and F. Parfitt and K. Poki-Walker and M. Farlow and A. Hake and B. Matthews and J. Brosch and S. Herring and C. V. van Dyck and A. Mecca and S. Good and M. MacAvoy and R. Carson and P. Varma and H. Chertkow and S. Vaitekunis and C. Hosein and S. Black and B. Stefanovic and C. Heyn and G. Hsiung and Ellen J. Kim and B. Mudge and V. Sossi and H. Feldman and M. Assaly and E. Finger and S. Pasternak and I. Rachinsky and A. Kertesz and D. Drost and J. Rogers and I. Grant and B. Muse and E. Rogalski and Jordan Robson and M. Mesulam and D. Kerwin and Chuang‐Kuo Wu and N. Johnson and K. Lipowski and S. Weintraub and B. Bonakdarpour and N. Pomara and R. Hernando and A. Sarrael and H. Rosen and B. Miller and D. Perry and Raymond Scott Turner and K. Johnson and B. Reynolds and Kelly Mccann and J. Poe and R. Sperling and Keith A. Johnson and G. Marshall and C. Belden and A. Atri and Bryan M. Spann and K. A. Clark and E. Zamrini and M. Sabbagh and R. Killiany and R. Stern and Jesse Mez and N. Kowall and A. Budson and T. Obisesan and Oyonumo E. Ntekim and S. Wolday and J. Khan and E. Nwulia and Sheeba Nadarajah and A. Lerner and P. Ogrocki and C. Tatsuoka and P. Fatica and Evan F. Fletcher and P. Maillard and J. Olichney and C. Decarli and Owen Carmichael and V. Bates and H. Capote and M. Rainka and M. Borrie and T. Lee and R. Bartha and Sterling C. Johnson and S. Asthana and C. Carlsson and A. Perrin and A. Burke and D. Scharre and M. Kataki and R. Tarawneh and Brendan Kelley and D. Hart and E. Zimmerman and D. Celmins and Delwyn D. Miller and L. L. Boles Ponto and Karen E. Smith and H. Koleva and H. Shim and K. W. Nam and S. Schultz and J. Williamson and S. Craft and J. Cleveland and Mia Yang and K. Sink and Brian L. Ott and J. Drake and G. Tremont and L. Daiello and J. Drake and M. Sabbagh and A. Ritter and C. Bernick and D. Munic and A. Mintz and Abigail O’Connelll and J. Mintzer and A. Wiliams and J. Masdeu and Jiong Shi and Angelica Garcia and M. Sabbagh and P. Newhouse and S. Potkin and S. Salloway and P. Malloy and S. Correia and S. Kittur and G. Pearlson and K. Blank and K. Anderson and L. Flashman and M. Seltzer and M. Hynes and R. Santulli and N. Relkin and G. Chiang and Michael P. Lin and L. Ravdin and Athena Lee and Ron Petersen and T. Neylan and J. Grafman and T. Montine and Sarah Danowski and Catherine Nguyen-Barrera and J. Hayes and Shannon Finley and Michael C. Donohue and M. Bernstein and M. Senjem and Normal L. Foster and T. Foroud and S. Potkin and L. Shen and K. Faber and Sungeun Kim and K. Nho and K. Wilmes and H. Vanderswag and A. Fleisher and Ajay Sood and K. Blanchard and D. Fleischman and K. Arfanakis and D. Varon and Maria T. Greig and B. Goldstein and K. Martin and G. Thai and Aimee L Pierce and C. Reist and B. Yáñez and E. Sosa and M. Witbracht and C. Sadowsky and Walter Martínez and Teresa Villena and H. Rosen and G. Marshall and E. Peskind and E. Petrie and Gail Li and J. Yesavage and Joy Taylor and Steven Z. Chao and J. Coleman and J. D. White and B. Lane and A. Rosen and J. Tinklenberg and S. Mackin and G. Jimenez-Maggiora and Erin Drake and Michael C. Donohue and C. Nelson and David Bickford and M. Butters and M. Zmuda and Bret J. Borowski and J. Gunter and M. Senjem and K. Kantarci and C. Ward and Denise Reyes and K. Faber and K. Nudelman and Yiu Ho Au and K. Scherer and Daniel Catalinotto and Samuel Stark and Elise Ong and Dariella Fernandez},
  year = {2021},
  abstract = {A biological research framework to define Alzheimer’ disease with dichotomized biomarker measurement was proposed by National Institute on Aging–Alzheimer’s Association (NIA–AA). However, it cannot characterize the hierarchy spreading pattern of tau pathology. To reflect in vivo tau progression using biomarker, we constructed a refined topographic 18 F-AV-1451 tau PET staging scheme with longitudinal clinical validation. Seven hundred and thirty-four participants with baseline 18 F-AV-1451 tau PET (baseline age 73.9 ± 7.7 years, 375 female) were stratified into five stages by a topographic PET staging scheme. Cognitive trajectories and clinical progression were compared across stages with or without further dichotomy of amyloid status, using linear mixed-effect models and Cox proportional hazard models. Significant cognitive decline was first observed in stage 1 when tau levels only increased in transentorhinal regions. Rates of cognitive decline and clinical progression accelerated from stage 2 to stage 3 and stage 4. Higher stages were also associated with greater CSF phosphorylated tau and total tau concentrations from stage 1. Abnormal tau accumulation did not appear with normal β-amyloid in neocortical regions but prompt cognitive decline by interacting with β-amyloid in temporal regions. Highly accumulated tau in temporal regions independently led to cognitive deterioration. Topographic PET staging scheme have potentials in early diagnosis, predicting disease progression, and studying disease mechanism. Characteristic tau spreading pattern in Alzheimer’s disease could be illustrated with biomarker measurement under NIA–AA framework. Clinical–neuroimaging–neuropathological studies in other cohorts are needed to validate these findings.},
  doi = {10.1038/s41398-021-01602-5},
  pmid = {34537810},
  month = {09},
  journal = {Translational Psychiatry},
  volume = {11},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
  note = {Cited by 60},
  pdf = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-021-01602-5.pdf},
}

@article{revell2021white,
  title = {White matter signals reflect information transmission between brain regions during seizures},
  author = {A. Revell and Marc Jaskir and Alfredo Lucas and Alexander B. Silva and Dhanya Mahesh and Lena Armstrong and T. Arnold and J. Bernabei and E. Gleichgerrcht and L. Bonilha and J. Stein and Sandhitsu R. Das and R. Shinohara and D. Bassett and B. Litt and K. Davis and See Mercier},
  year = {2021},
  abstract = {White matter supports critical brain functions such as learning and memory, modulates the distribution of action potentials, and transmits neural information between brain regions. Notably, neuronal cell bodies exist in deeper white matter tissue, neurotransmitter vesicles are released directly in white matter, and white matter blood-oxygenation level dependent (BOLD) signals are detectable across a range of different tasks—all appearing to reflect a dynamic, active tissue from which recorded signals can reveal meaningful information about the brain. Yet, the signals within white matter have largely been ignored. Here, we elucidate the properties of white matter signals using intracranial EEG in a bipolar montage. We show that such signals capture the communication between brain regions and differentiate pathophysiologies of epilepsy. In direct contradiction to past assumptions that white matter functional signals provide little value, we show that white matter recordings can elucidate brain function and pathophysiology. Broadly, white matter functional recordings acquired through implantable devices may provide a wealth of currently untapped knowledge about the neurobiology of disease.},
  doi = {10.1101/2021.09.15.460549},
  pmid = {41340548},
  month = {09},
  journal = {Brain},
  volume = {149},
  pages = {77 - 89},
  altmetric = {true},
  dimensions = {true},
  note = {Cited by 19},
  pdf = {https://www.biorxiv.org/content/biorxiv/early/2021/09/17/2021.09.15.460549.full.pdf},
}

@article{schwartz2021fully,
